{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments ["<b>A woman of beautiful appearance.</b> Scripture spoke in terms of the present case. And in the Sifrei (Ki Teitzei 5): from where do we know even if she is ugly? Scripture states, <b>and you desire her</b> (וחשקת בה) — even though she is not of beautiful appearance. And R. Abraham [Ibn Ezra] said (on Deuteronomy 21:11), <b>of beautiful appearance</b> — in his eyes. And this is joined with <b>and you see</b> (וראית). And Scripture mentioned <b>and you desire her</b>, so as not to permit her to him solely on account of desire, when his impulse overpowers him; but if he finds in himself that he has no desire for intercourse with her, but only wishes to take a wife, he must not marry this one. And thus they said in the Sifrei (there), <b>and you take her for yourself as a wife</b> — do not say, this one is for my father, this one is for my brother. And in the Gemara (Kiddushin 22a), that he must not take two women, one for his son and one for himself, one for his father and one for himself. And the prohibition regarding the one [designated] for his son applies even to her alone."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {0 "The phrase 'joined with וראית' (והנה הוא דבק עם וראית) refers to Ibn Ezra's linking of the two criteria — seeing and desiring — as both being subjective to the individual."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T02:52:29.821698533Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}